Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:10.570 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  as a spiritual successor to banjo  kazooie ukulele really does capture the  cheeky personality of its predecessor it  doesn't introduce many new ideas but it  does rework the existing formula  creating a more open and far less linear  version of the n64 collectathon  ultimately though bloated levels and a  largely uncooperative camera keep  ukulele from being more than just a  nostalgia trip the adventure begins when  corporate president capital B steals  yuca and Laelius golden pages so of  course you have to enter his building to  get them back that hub area Highbury  towers listen to five steamed worlds but  the themes aren't terribly original for  banjo games or games in general and like  in banjo you have a variety of  collectables to find wherever you go  from pay G's to currency like coils to  power up ukulele is about finding  collectables first and foremost and  platforming takes somewhat of a backseat  there is more than one page e unlocked  by playing around a skull several found  by completing races and many hidden  behind simple memory puzzles to name a  few that's actually a good thing because  the camera is often very uncooperative  it locks into place at seemingly random  points and even swings wildly in tough  spots making the platforming challenges  that are they're terribly frustrating  and time-consuming  [Music]  when most of your time goes toward the  collectibles that are the least fun to  get that the problem once I unlock the  ability to fly for a limited periods of  time I started cheating completely to  get around platforming I didn't want to  do I most consistently enjoyed the hub  world where the power of collectibles  are hidden in clever spots and the pages  are locked behind challenging puzzles  that make good use of ukuleles moveset  and strategies you pick up in the actual  world of course the fact that the hub  area stands out isn't a good thing for  the rest of you Glalie each of the  levels can be expanded once which is a  new take on the formula and you can  visit each of them in pretty much any  order you want that open structure makes  for a steady pace since you don't have  to stay stuck on a page your puzzle you  can move on to another world where you  might learn a move or strategy that will  help you elsewhere that also makes  returning to a world you've already  visited more rewarding when you found 20  of the 25 pages in the world and we're  sure there was nothing else you could  possibly do to find the rest coming back  to that area with fresh eyes and new  abilities under your belt and seeing  what you didn't before is its own  special kind of achievement the other  update to the banjo formula of being  able to expand worlds is less successful  each of the base worlds is clearly  incomplete but expanding them doesn't  necessarily improve them because most of  them have additions in multiple areas  and directions their layouts are hard to  follow and it can be tedious just to  figure out where you haven't been yet  the snow level in particular is broken  up into a bunch of areas that connect in  bizarre ways and even after several  hours exploring it I'm not entirely sure  which caves connect to which part twenty  hours of hunting collectibles and  fighting simple bosses later I had found  the bare minimum amount of pages to move  on to the end of the game and couldn't  justify finding any more than that the  ones I had left were not worth either  the time it would take or the  frustration I'd have to endure to get  them I stayed as long as I did because  of my own nostalgia although there  aren't references outright ukelele has  plenty of banjo parallels the lizard and  bat duo are nearly identical in  personality to Banjo and Kazooie with  lately making the majority of the snarky  comments and Yuka being the voice of  reason  on top of that the collectibles and  moves are very similar in function to  their counterparts in the banjo games  and the music features many of the same  familiar motif but ukulele also lacks  new ideas and the twist introduces or  entirely successful ultimately ukuleles  best and worst aspects come directly  from its predecessor if you're looking  for a faithful return to the banjo  kazooie formula ukulele certainly  delivers from the fonts the menus to the  music it's worthy of the title of  spiritual successor but despite attempts  at modernizing the formula its style of  gameplay is still outdated and it  doesn't stay challenging or interesting  for long because  [Music]

